L["creation-light"] = """The Bible begins with the most important sentence ever written. In the beginning, God created the heavens and the earth. Before there was a sun, a sky, or even time, there was God. Everything else has a beginning. He doesn't.

This was written for a people who were surrounded by neighbors telling very different stories. In those stories the world came out of a fight between gods, and people were made as slaves to do the gods' work. Genesis says something completely different. One God, with no rival, spoke, and everything came to be.

At first the earth was empty and dark and covered with water. Then God said, let there be light, and there was light. He separated the light from the darkness. He called the light day and the darkness night. That was the first day.

On the next days God made the sky, then the dry land and the seas, and he filled the land with plants and trees. He made the sun, the moon, and the stars to mark days and seasons. He filled the water with fish and the sky with birds. Then he made every animal on the land.

After each step the Bible says, God saw that it was good. Creation was not an accident or a mistake. God made it and he loved it.

On the sixth day he did something different. He said, let us make mankind in our image. Men and women are the only part of creation described this way. Every human being, of every age and race, carries something of God. He blessed them and told them to take care of the earth.

On the seventh day God rested. He blessed that day and made it holy. This is where the Sabbath comes from. God was not tired. He was showing us that life is not only work. It also has rest and worship in it.

Why does this matter? Because it tells us who we are and who God is. The world was made on purpose, by a good God, and so were you. John's gospel begins with the same words, in the beginning, and says that Jesus was there too. All things were made through him.

What happened after? The very next chapter shows a man and a woman in a garden, and then it shows how they lost it. But the world God made was never thrown away. The Bible ends with God making all things new, and the light comes back for good.

What does this mean for us today? It means you are not an accident. You were made by someone who looked at what he had made and called it good. That includes you, even on the days you don't feel good.

It also means that darkness is not the end of the story. God's first act was to speak light into the dark. He still does that. When life feels empty, shapeless, and dark, the same God is able to say a word and begin again.

That is why this story is so important. It is the foundation of everything else in the Bible. If God made everything, then everything belongs to him, including your life, your worries, and your future. You can start every day knowing that you were made on purpose, and that the one who made you is still speaking."""

L["creation-eve"] = """After God made the sky, the land, the sea, and every animal, he made the first man. The Bible says God formed him from the dust of the ground and breathed life into his nose. His name was Adam, which is close to the Hebrew word for ground. He was alive because God breathed into him.

God planted a garden in a place called Eden and put Adam there to take care of it. It had rivers, trees full of fruit, and everything he needed. God was close, and walked with them in the garden. It was the way the world was meant to be.

God said that Adam could eat from every tree in the garden except one, the tree of the knowledge of good and evil. That was the only rule. Adam also got his first job. God brought every animal to him, and Adam named each one.

Then comes a surprising line. For the first time in the story, God says something is not good. It is not good for the man to be alone. Adam had a perfect garden, a perfect job, and God himself nearby, and still the Bible says he needed another person. We were made for each other.

Among all the animals, no partner was found for him. So God made Adam fall into a deep sleep. He took part of the man's side, and from it he made a woman. The word for side is the same word used for the side of the ark and the side of the tabernacle. Many readers have noticed that she was not taken from his head to rule over him or from his feet to be walked on, but from his side, to stand beside him.

God brought her to Adam, and Adam was so happy that the first poem in the Bible came out of his mouth. At last, he said, this is bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh. He called her woman. Later he would name her Eve, which means life.

Then the writer adds a sentence about us. This is why a man leaves his father and mother and is united to his wife, and they become one flesh. Jesus quoted this verse himself when people asked him about marriage. He said it goes back to the beginning, and what God has joined, no one should separate.

The Bible says the man and woman were naked and felt no shame. They had nothing to hide from each other or from God. That is how close they were.

Why does this matter? It shows that marriage and family came from God. Before there were kings, cities, or temples, there was a man and a woman and a promise that they belonged to each other. It also shows that men and women are equal. They are made of the same stuff and both carry God's image.

What happened after? Right after this scene comes the garden's greatest test. They would soon throw all this away, and we will see how in the next scene. Even so, God did not give up on marriage or on people. The Bible ends with a wedding feast.

What does this mean for us today? If you are lonely, this story says that is not a flaw in you. God made people to need people. Wanting love and friendship is part of how he built you.

It also says that relationships are a gift to treasure, not something to take for granted. The first good thing in human life was not a skill or a job. It was another person who would stand beside you.

That is why this story is so important. It tells us where love, marriage, and family come from, and who they belong to. Whether you are married, single, or hoping, the God who looked at Adam and said it is not good to be alone still pays attention to who is beside you."""

L["eden"] = """In the garden, God had given Adam and Eve everything. They could eat from any tree except one. God told them that if they ate from the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, they would die. It was a rule given by someone who loved them and wanted to keep them safe.

Then a serpent showed up. The Bible says it was the most clever of all the wild animals. Later writers in the Bible say that behind the serpent was Satan, God's enemy. He didn't start with a lie that was easy to spot. He started with a question. Did God really say, you must not eat from any tree in the garden?

That question twisted what God had said. God had said they could eat from all the trees but one. The serpent made it sound as if God was holding something back. Eve corrected him a little, but she added to the rule. Then the serpent said it straight. You will not die. God knows that when you eat it your eyes will be opened, and you will be like God.

Eve looked at the fruit. It looked good to eat and good to look at, and it seemed like a way to be wise. The Bible doesn't say it was an apple. It just says fruit. She took some, ate it, and gave some to her husband, who was with her. He ate it too.

Right away, the promise of the serpent turned out to be false. Their eyes were opened, but what they saw was that they were naked. Shame was new to them. They sewed fig leaves together to cover themselves. And when they heard God walking in the garden in the cool of the day, they hid.

God called out, where are you? He knew where they were. He was asking them to come out. Adam said he was afraid. God asked if he had eaten from the tree. Adam blamed Eve, and he even hinted at blaming God, the woman you put here with me. Eve blamed the serpent. Nobody said, I did it.

God spoke to the serpent first. He said that one of Eve's descendants would one day crush the serpent's head, even though the serpent would strike his heel. Christians have read this as the first promise of Jesus, who defeated evil by dying and rising. God spoke about the future even in his anger.

Then God told Adam and Eve what sin would cost them. Life would now include pain, hard work, and death. He sent them out of the garden. The Bible says God made clothes of animal skin for them before they left. Even in judgment he took care of them. He placed angels and a flaming sword at the entrance to guard the way to the tree of life.

Why does this matter? This is the story of how everything broke. It explains why there is pain, work that is hard, and death. It also shows that the first sin was not about fruit. It was about not trusting that God is good, and about wanting to decide for ourselves what is right.

What happened after? The very next chapter has their son killing his brother. Sin spread from there to everyone. But God's promise to send a rescuer kept the story moving. The whole Bible is the story of that promise coming true. At the end, in Revelation, the tree of life comes back and the way is open again.

What does this mean for us today? We still live east of Eden. We still hide when we do something wrong. We still blame someone else. It helps to see that this goes all the way back, and that God's first question is not a threat. It is where are you? He is still looking for us.

It also shows how temptation works. It usually starts by making us doubt that God is good, and that he really wants the best for us. When you notice that doubt, remember that the serpent's first move was a question.

That is why this story is so important. It tells us the truth about ourselves, and it also tells us the truth about God. Even when his children broke the one rule, he came looking for them, covered them, and promised a rescue. That promise was kept."""

L["cain-abel"] = """Adam and Eve had left the garden, and life was now hard. They had two sons. The first was Cain, and he worked the soil. The second was Abel, who took care of sheep. Eve was so thankful at Cain's birth that she said, with the help of the Lord I have brought forth a man.

One day both brothers brought an offering to God. Cain brought some of the crops he had grown. Abel brought the first and best of his flock. The Bible says God looked with favor on Abel and his offering, but not on Cain and his. It doesn't say exactly why. The New Testament says Abel brought his offering by faith. It seems Abel gave his best with a trusting heart, and Cain gave something and expected it to be enough.

Cain was furious, and his face fell. God spoke to him directly. He asked why he was angry. Then he gave him a warning that is one of the most important lines in Genesis. Sin is crouching at your door. It wants to have you, but you must rule over it. God was not rejecting Cain. He was telling him there was still a way to do what is right.

Cain didn't listen. He said to his brother, let's go out to the field. When they were there, he attacked Abel and killed him. It was the first death of a human being recorded in the Bible, and it was a murder. It happened within one generation of the garden.

God asked Cain, where is your brother Abel? Cain lied. I don't know. Am I my brother's keeper? He was trying to cover it up, just as his parents hid in the garden. But God said, what have you done? Listen. Your brother's blood cries out to me from the ground.

The ground itself would no longer give crops for Cain. He would become a wanderer. Cain cried out that the punishment was more than he could bear. Anyone who found him might kill him. And then God did something unexpected. He put a mark on Cain so no one would kill him. Even a murderer was still under God's care.

Cain went away from the presence of the Lord and settled east of Eden. He had a wife, and he built a city. Adam and Eve had another son named Seth, and the Bible says it was in his family that people began to call on the name of the Lord.

Why does this matter? It shows how quickly sin grows. One bite in the garden led to one murder in the field. It also shows the way sin works. God warned Cain, and the anger was a choice before it was an action. The New Testament says Cain belonged to the evil one, and that anyone who hates a brother is like him.

What happened after? Abel's name lives on. Hebrews says that by faith he still speaks, even though he is dead. Jesus called him righteous. The blood that cried from the ground is contrasted in Hebrews with the blood of Jesus, which speaks of forgiveness instead of revenge.

What does this mean for us today? Most of us will never kill anyone. But we know what it feels like when someone gets what we wanted, and our face falls. God's words to Cain are for us too. Anger is not a sin by itself, but it can crouch at the door. You can rule over it, with God's help.

The story also asks the question every one of us has to answer. Am I my brother's keeper? The Bible's answer is yes. Jesus said the second great commandment is to love your neighbor as yourself. We are responsible for the people around us.

That is why this story is so important. It shows the first family after Eden, broken by envy, and it shows that God still speaks to people before they do wrong, and still protects them after. He gave Cain a warning and a way out. He gives us the same."""

L["flood"] = """By the time of Noah, the world had gotten very bad. The Bible says the earth was full of violence, and that every thought of the human heart was only evil all the time. God was grieved. This wasn't a sudden fit of anger. It broke his heart to see what people had done to each other and to the world he had made.

But there was one man who stood out. Noah was a righteous man, and he walked with God. Noah was also the descendant of Seth, the family that had begun to call on the Lord. God decided to start over, and he chose Noah to make that possible.

God told Noah to build an ark, a huge wooden boat. The Bible gives the size as 300 cubits long, 50 wide, and 30 tall. That is about 450 feet long, longer than a football field and a half. He was to cover it with pitch inside and out. Noah would have to build it on dry land, far from any sea, while his neighbors watched.

The Bible says that Noah did everything God commanded. Peter's second letter calls him a preacher of righteousness. It seems that for the years he was building, he was also warning people. No one listened. We aren't told exactly how long it took. Many have said, going back to old Jewish and Christian writers, that it took a hundred years or more, but the Bible doesn't say exactly.

When the ark was done, God told Noah to bring his wife, his three sons and their wives, and pairs of every kind of animal. Eight people in all. The animals came to him. Then the Lord himself shut the door.

Then the rain began. The springs of the great deep burst open and the windows of heaven were opened. It rained for forty days and forty nights. The water rose until it covered even the highest mountains. Everything that had the breath of life on dry land died. Only Noah and those with him in the ark were left. The water stayed on the earth for 150 days.

This part of the Bible is hard to read. But the story keeps repeating one thing. God remembered Noah. When everything seems lost, the next scene begins with those words. God remembered Noah and all the animals that were with him. He sent a wind over the earth, and the water began to go down.

Why does this matter? It shows that God takes evil seriously, and also that he saves. Peter says the flood is a picture of baptism, where the water saves by raising the people in the ark above judgment. Jesus said that the days before his return would be like the days of Noah, with people carrying on as normal and not paying attention.

What happened after? The ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat. Noah stepped out into a washed world. In the next scene we will see him send out a dove. After the flood, God made a promise to Noah and to everything living. He would never again destroy the earth with water. He set his bow in the clouds as the sign.

What does this mean for us today? It means that God sees what is going on. He is not blind to cruelty. He is patient, and gave Noah a hundred years or so to build and warn, but he is also just.

It also means there is a way of rescue. The ark had one door. Jesus said, I am the door. Noah's family didn't save themselves. They did the one thing God asked, they went in, and God shut the door behind them.

That is why this story is so important. It is not about a boat and animals. It is about a God who is grieved by evil, who judges it, and who still keeps a faithful few safe. When you feel like the only person trying to do right, remember that God noticed Noah."""
